In Java, you should not use the `==` operator to compare strings. Instead, you should use the `equals()` method. Here's why:

- `==` operator compares the memory addresses of two objects. If two strings are stored in different memory locations, `==` will return `false`, even if their contents are the same.
- `equals()` method compares the contents of two strings. If two strings have the same characters in the same order, `equals()` will return `true`, regardless of their memory locations.

Using `==` to compare strings can lead to unexpected results and bugs, especially when dealing with string manipulation or concatenation. For example, consider the following code:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";
String str3 = new String("hello");

if (str1 == str2) { // true
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are equal");
}

if (str1 == str3) { // false
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 are equal");
}

if (str1.equals(str2)) { // true
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are equal (using equals())");
}

if (str1.equals(str3)) { // true
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 are equal (using equals())");
}
```

In this example, `str1` and `str2` are stored in the same memory location, so `str1 == str2` returns `true`. However, `str1` and `str3` are stored in different memory locations, so `str1 == str3` returns `false`. On the other hand, `str1.equals(str2)` and `str1.equals(str3)` both return `true`, because they compare the contents of the strings, not their memory locations.

To summarize, you should always use the `equals()` method to compare strings in Java. It is a reliable and safe way to compare strings, regardless of their memory locations. Using `==` to compare strings can lead to unexpected results and bugs, especially when dealing with string manipulation or concatenation.

